New in this repository: the batched recursion and address lemma for the exact-DFT model
(notes/batched-dft-note.tex, Lemmas 2.2-2.3 and Theorem 3.1), the normal-form lemma as used here
(Lemma 4.1), an independent exact re-check of the moment (scripts/verify_moment.py,
certificates/moment.json; the upstream repository certifies the same moment), and the
observation that the network
below applies to the exact DFT. The batched accounting itself was introduced for integer
multiplication by icekylinx in CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds.

The finite network is the paired-cube complex supplier of CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds:
  https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds
  commit d1d6c070f5a8c684727ee7ec35d930f9ebfa9758 (merged PR #144 integration)
certificates/network-children.json is derived from that repository's
certificates/paired-cube-complex-input.json (sha256 recorded in SOURCES.json), which is
Apache-2.0. Its NOTICE, CONTRIBUTORS.md and docs/research/community-round6-review.md at that
commit give the authoritative attribution for the network, and all of it applies here.
Principal contributors to this network: icekylinx (paired cubes, signed channels,
coordinate-star centers, frame and carrier compiler, gauges, arbitrary-subspace Clifford frames,
three-stage covers, copied retained centers, endpoint gauges, batched recursion; with OpenAI
GPT-6 Astra and Codex assistance); an664 (completed-core sharing, PR #128; with OpenAI Codex
assistance); eumemic (positive producer DAG, PR #117, and earlier complex networks and source
frames; with Claude assistance); Aurel Prosz (Paureel); Zhihao Chen (jacklightChen); Swapnil
Jain; dleen (retained totals and stage sharing, PR #4); Douglas Colkitt (repository, framework, integration and review); and OpenAI
(original complex phase network). Other contributors credited there for this lineage retain
that credit. None of them has reviewed this repository.

The exact-DFT framework whose Sections 3-5 are reused is OpenAI, "An explicit power saving for
the exact discrete Fourier transform" (25 September 2026), https://github.com/openai/math,
commit adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a. No text or code from it is copied here.
